1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,816
JAMIE: Victoria's Secret jumped forty percent yesterday. Yes—that Victoria's Secret. We're going to explain how a company America spent a decade roasting became the market's favorite comeback story.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/02/victorias-secret-vsxy-earnings-q1-2026.html

2
00:00:08,816 --> 00:00:40,617
ALEX: So Victoria's Secret jumped forty percent yesterday—turns out the internet's favorite punching bag figured out how to actually sell things to humans instead of selling a fantasy that made humans feel bad. The real story though: new leadership brought in a completely different design team, killed the Angel sizing that was basically a cry for help, and the brand actually started making bras that fit women's actual bodies instead of, you know, a fever dream. Same company, different product. We'll find out in earnings whether this is structural or just a bounce that evaporates the moment they miss a quarter.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/02/victorias-secret-vsxy-earnings-q1-2026.html

3
00:00:40,617 --> 00:01:15,253
ALEX: Markets are closed today, so we're recapping Tuesday's close. The S&P 500 eked out point one three percent, Russell 2000 crushed it at point nine zero percent—small caps actually outpacing the big boys for once. But here's the thing that should worry you: we're on a nine-day winning streak and fewer stocks are participating. It's like a concert where the band keeps playing louder but half the audience is already heading to the parking lot. Cisco jumped five point five zero percent on earnings, Microsoft dropped four point one seven percent, and the V I X fell one point seven four percent because apparently nothing bad exists on Tuesdays.

4
00:01:15,253 --> 00:01:25,958
JAMIE: Wait—Microsoft down four point one seven percent while the overall market's up? That's the breadth warning you're talking about, right? The index is being held up by what, Cisco and a few others?

5
00:01:25,958 --> 00:01:56,500
JAMIE: So Victoria's Secret raised its full-year sales outlook after beating earnings, and the stock response was that forty percent jump. But here's what actually matters: the company walked into a situation that was basically a museum of early-2010s marketing mistakes—the Angel brand was built on the idea that women should want to look like a specific type of person, and that specific type of person was getting smaller every season. They've now completely rebuilt the bra sizing, killed the fantasy-first messaging, and started selling to women who actually, you know, exist in normal human body shapes.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/02/victorias-secret-vsxy-earnings-q1-2026.html

6
00:01:56,500 --> 00:02:32,080
ALEX: Right, so they stopped selling aspiration and started selling compression. The market's rewarding them for it because the earnings actually showed it works—comparable sales up, margins expanding, younger customers coming back. But I need to pump the brakes here: one quarter of good numbers after a decade of decline isn't structural change, it's a relief rally. The stock was trading at fire-sale valuations. If they miss next quarter, this entire narrative inverts and we're back to 'legacy apparel brand in secular decline.' The real test is whether this company can sustain a turnaround or whether they just caught the market at a moment when anything that beats is getting a victory lap.

7
00:02:32,080 --> 00:03:02,622
MAYA: Okay but the actual cultural shift here is insane—like, for years Victoria's Secret was the brand that made women feel like they were shopping at a store designed by someone who'd only ever seen women in a magazine. Now they're the brand that's like, 'Hey, your body is fine, here's a bra that fits it.' That's not a product change, that's a complete inversion of the business model. And the young customers are showing up for it. Gen Z doesn't want to be sold a fantasy, they want to be sold confidence that they're already enough.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/02/victorias-secret-vsxy-earnings-q1-2026.html

8
00:03:02,622 --> 00:03:18,995
ALEX: Palo Alto Networks upgraded after earnings—cybersecurity is now non-negotiable infrastructure, not a discretionary spend. Their leadership said the latest AI advancements have actually increased the urgency around security, which is the first time in two years I've heard someone use AI as a reason to buy something instead of panic-sell everything.

9
00:03:18,995 --> 00:03:37,887
MAYA: Wait, so the AI apocalypse narrative is finally hitting a wall because it turns out when you're building systems that could theoretically end civilization, you need really good locks on the door? That's like—that's like finding out your house is on fire and being like, 'Well, at least now I have a good reason to upgrade the security system.'

10
00:03:37,887 --> 00:03:52,371
ALEX: Ulta Beauty beat and raised on earnings—beauty retail is having a moment while big tech stumbles. Comparable sales up, margins expanding in a category that was supposed to be Amazon-proof. And it turns out it actually is, because you can't buy lipstick from a box.

11
00:03:52,371 --> 00:04:04,021
JAMIE: So while Microsoft is down, Ulta is up. A company selling mascara is outperforming a company selling artificial intelligence. I'm not saying that's the entire market telling us something, but I'm also not not saying that.

12
00:04:04,021 --> 00:04:18,505
MAYA: Microsoft just kicked off Build 2026 with a keynote full of AI integration announcements—new Surface hardware, new models, the whole stack. Investors immediately voted 'we've heard this before' by selling stock. It's like watching someone do a perfect backflip into a pool that's already empty.
  ↳ source: https://www.theverge.com/tech/941738/microsoft-build-2026-biggest-announcements

13
00:04:18,505 --> 00:04:35,508
ALEX: Fed Chair Warsh just made his first hires, and one of them wrote the Fed chapter in Project 2025. So the central bank is about to become a lot more political, which is either structural policy reform or regulatory theater, depending on whether you trust that anyone in Washington knows the difference anymore.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/02/fed-chair-warsh-makes-first-hires-at-central-bank-including-project-2025-author.html

14
00:04:35,508 --> 00:04:57,863
MAYA: Sailors have been trapped in the Strait of Hormuz for months because of the Iran war zone blockade, and the psychological toll is real—we're talking stress, exhaustion, crews that haven't seen their families since before the election. One ship's captain said the uncertainty is worse than the actual danger, which is what happens when you turn human beings into floating collateral in a geopolitical dispute they didn't sign up for.

15
00:04:57,863 --> 00:05:23,682
ALEX: So maritime insurance premiums have tripled, shipping routes are rerouting around Africa adding forty days to transit times, and the global supply chain is basically playing four-dimensional chess to avoid a strait that handles thirty percent of the world's oil. Meanwhile the sailors themselves are stuck in what amounts to a floating hostage negotiation, except nobody's negotiating for them. The economic cost is measured in billions. The human cost is measured in people who haven't called their kids in six months.

16
00:05:23,682 --> 00:05:35,962
ALEX: Victoria's Secret went from being the punchline everyone quoted to posting a forty percent day, which means the market just paid significant money to find out that women are capable of buying things when they're not being insulted.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/06/02/victorias-secret-vsxy-earnings-q1-2026.html

17
00:05:35,962 --> 00:05:42,889
MAYA: They basically hired leadership who looked at years of 'aspiration' marketing and said no thanks, I'll sell bras instead of mythology.

18
00:05:42,889 --> 00:05:47,297
JAMIE: This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
